\rem source Jane Ellen Harrison, Prolegomena to the Study of Greek Religion (Cambridge, 1903), Critical Appendix on the Orphic Tablets by Gilbert Murray, pp. 659-674: archive.org scans prolegomenatostu00harruoft and prolegomenatostu00harrrich (OCR, collated and checked against the page images)
\rem rights Public domain (published 1903)
\h Orphic Gold Tablets
\toc1 Orphic Gold Tablets
\mt1 Orphic Gold Tablets
\c 1
\p
\v 1 \vp I\vp* Thou shalt find to the left of the House of Hades a Well-spring,
\q1 And by the side thereof standing a white cypress.
\q1 To this Well-spring approach not near.
\q1 But thou shalt find another by the Lake of Memory,
\q1 Cold water flowing forth, and there are Guardians before it.
\q1 Say: “I am a child of Earth and of Starry Heaven;
\q1 But my race is of Heaven (alone). This ye know yourselves.
\q1 And lo, I am parched with thirst and I perish. Give me quickly
\q1 The cold water flowing forth from the Lake of Memory.”
\q1 And of themselves they will give thee to drink from the holy Well-spring,
\q1 And thereafter among the other Heroes thou shalt have lordship....
\v 2 \vp II\vp* I am parched with thirst and I perish.—Nay, drink of Me,
\q1 The well-spring flowing for ever on the Right, where the Cypress is.
\q1 Who art thou?..................
\q1 Whence art thou?—I am son of Earth and of Starry Heaven.
\v 3 \vp III\vp* But so soon as the Spirit hath left the light of the sun,
\q1 To the right.......................................of Ennoia
\q1 Then must man...............being right wary in all things.
\q1 Hail, thou who hast suffered the Suffering. This thou hadst never suffered before.
\q1 Thou art become God from Man. A kid thou art fallen into milk.
\q1 Hail, hail to thee journeying on the right.........
\q1 ...Holy meadows and groves of Phersephoneia.
\v 4 \vp IV\vp* O First-Born, Earth, Counsellor, All-Motherly, Cybelean, Kora, Holy Child of Demeter(?)......, All-Seeing Zeus, Healer, and thou Sarapis, Sun, Fire-Kindler, Maker-of-Appearances, Far-Seeing (??)
\p Victory and equal Fortune; come ye, Phanes, All-Counselling Fates (or With victories and Fortunes thou didst appear, with the All-Devising Fates)
\p Stayers (?), All-Accomplishers (?), Well-named Daemon, Master, Healer (?), All-Subduer, All-Controller, Driver of Thunder, Sickle-Bearer (?), ............to be endured in all wise. That thou mayest not with vapour make to burn a tumour in me (??) ... I will pay ... sevenfold fasting. In the nights or after daybreak I ...... for seven days the fasting.
\p Zeus Penetrator (?) and All-seeing, Divine, Ruler of Streams, ... ye will make to spring a stream not in drops of fire.........
\p Plain ...... guide ... Divine Rhadamanthys ...... for six days...... Zeus ...... Demeter ...... Healer, Sun ...... that She sharing the Shrine for five days may not..................
\v 5 \vp V\vp* Out of the Pure I come, Pure Queen of Them Below,
\q1 And Eukles and Eubouleus, and other Gods and Daemons:
\q1 For I also avow me that I am of your blessed race.
\q1 And I have paid the penalty for deeds unrighteous,
\q1 Whether it be that Fate laid me low or the Gods Immortal
\q1 Or .................. with star-flung thunderbolt.
\q1 I have flown out of the sorrowful weary Wheel;
\q1 I have passed with eager feet to the Circle desired;
\q1 I have sunk beneath the bosom of Despoina, Queen of the Underworld;
\q1 I have passed with eager feet to (or from) the Circle desired;
\q1 And now I come a suppliant to Holy Phersephoneia
\q1 That of her grace she receive me to the seats of the Hallowed.—
\q1 Happy and Blessèd One, thou shalt be God instead of Mortal.
\v 6 \vp VIII\vp* She comes from the Pure, O Pure Queen of those below
\q1 And Eukles and Eubouleus.—Child of Zeus, receive here the armour
\q1 Of Memory, (’tis a gift songful among men)
\q1 Thou Caecilia Secundina, in due rite to avert evil for ever.
